    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d644">

                <pb facs="0697" n="689" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg689"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War, 1939/441: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Rumania</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gunther</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bucharest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-09-19T21:00:00+03:00">September 19, 1939—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 20—9:02 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>226. Following from Biddle:<anchor n="37" xml:id="d644-f1"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“16. 1. Rumanian Minister of Foreign Affairs<anchor n="38"
                                xml:id="d644-f2"/> informed Polish Ambassador here this morning that
                            Berlin had warned Bucharest to the effect that Berlin would not
                            countenance Bucharest’s (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) permitting
                            officials of the Polish Government to function or (<hi
                                rendition="#italic">b</hi>) permitting officials or Polish refugees
                            of military age to leave Rumania.</p>
                        <p>2. Accordingly under German pressure Rumanian Government has interned
                            President Moscicki and other Polish Government officials and Marshal
                                Smigly-Rydz.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d644-f3"/></p>
                        <p>3. While Marshal’s internment is covered by Hague Convention of
                                1907<anchor n="40" xml:id="d644-f4"/> I am aware of no precedent for
                            the internment of the Government of a belligerent country if that
                            Government seeks transit through a neutral country. If therefore my
                            assumption is correct in this regard Rumania’s internment of Polish
                            Government officials would represent a violation of international
                            law.</p>
                        <p>4. If under these circumstances the Polish Government reaches an impasse
                            in effecting its safe conduct through Rumania it is conceivable that it
                            may appoint a provisional government in France, resign, proceed to
                            France as private citizens and there resume its official status by
                            reappointment by the provisional government.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
